```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 6,
                      fig.height = 4, dpi = 150)
inla_ok <- requireNamespace("INLA", quietly = TRUE)
brms_ok <- requireNamespace("brms", quietly = TRUE)
agri_ok <- requireNamespace("agridat", quietly = TRUE)
options(flexyBayes.silence_default_prior_note = TRUE)

# knitr::kable(format = "markdown") escapes a pipe to &#124; so it cannot
# break the table, and pandoc then prints that entity literally inside a
# code span, so `(x | g)` renders as `(x &#124; g)`. Emitting HTML
# directly, with backticks turned into <code> here, keeps the pipe.
.md_code <- function(x) {
  x <- gsub("&", "&amp;", x, fixed = TRUE)
  x <- gsub("<", "&lt;",  x, fixed = TRUE)
  x <- gsub(">", "&gt;",  x, fixed = TRUE)
  gsub("`([^`]+)`", "<code>\\1</code>", x)
}
.cap_table <- function(df) {
  df[] <- lapply(df, .md_code)
  knitr::kable(df, format = "html", escape = FALSE)
}
```

<div class="fb-box">
<span class="fb-box-title">This page in one panel</span>
<dl>
<dt>The problem</dt>
<dd>Yield of 18 wheat genotypes across 9 environments. Which genotypes
are good, and how sure are we?</dd>
<dt>In ASReml</dt>
<dd><code>asreml(yield ~ env, random = ~ gen, data = dat)</code></dd>
<dt>In flexyBayes</dt>
<dd><code>flexybayes(yield ~ env, random = ~ gen, data = dat)</code></dd>
<dt>What the posterior adds</dt>
<dd>Every quantity arrives as a distribution rather than an estimate and
a standard error, so an interval for a variance component needs no
appeal to large-sample theory, and "how sure are we" is answered by
counting draws.</dd>
<dt>What it costs</dt>
<dd>Seconds instead of milliseconds, a prior you can inspect, and a
smaller set of model structures than ASReml offers.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

# 1. What changes when you go Bayesian

A REML fit answers with a number and an uncertainty attached to it: the
genotype variance is 0.11, with a standard error. A Bayesian fit answers
with a distribution over that variance, and the number you quote is a
summary of it.

That difference matters in three practical places.

**Variance components near zero.** REML puts the estimate at the
boundary and the standard error becomes hard to interpret, because the
sampling theory behind it assumes the estimate is not sitting on the
edge of the parameter space. A posterior has no boundary problem: it is
simply concentrated near zero, and its upper credible bound is a real
statement about how large the component could plausibly be.

**Small numbers of levels.** With nine environments, asymptotic
intervals for the between-environment variance are optimistic. The
posterior is whatever the data and the prior support, at any sample
size.

**Functions of parameters.** Heritability, a ratio of variance
components, needs the delta method under REML. Under a posterior it is
one line: compute the ratio on every draw and summarise.

Against that, three things are genuinely harder. Fitting takes seconds
to minutes rather than milliseconds. A prior exists whether or not you
choose it, so it has to be reported. And the structures on offer are
narrower than ASReml's, which is the subject of Section 2 and of the
*formula surface* vignette.

# 2. Two engines, one grammar

flexyBayes does not implement an inference algorithm. It parses a model
written in ASReml-style or brms-style syntax and hands it to one of two
established engines.

**INLA** approximates the posterior deterministically, by nested Laplace
approximation. It is fast, it has no chains to converge, and it is built
around latent Gaussian models -- which is why the structured terms
(autoregressive fields, splines, known covariance matrices) live here.

**brms** writes a Stan program and samples it with Hamiltonian Monte
Carlo. It is slower and its answer carries Monte Carlo error, but it is
not restricted to the latent Gaussian class, which is why the flexible
likelihoods and the interaction structures live there.

They are complementary rather than interchangeable. The table below is
generated from the package's own capability record, so it cannot drift
from what the code does.

```{r capability, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
m <- flexyBayes:::.fb_capability_matrix()
.cap_table(data.frame(
  `Model class` = m$model_class, Spelling = m$spelling,
  INLA = m$inla, brms = m$brms, check.names = FALSE
))
```

Three readings of that table are worth having before you fit anything.

*`fits`* means an active engine emits the structure and a test exercises
it. *`refuses`* means the request raises an error naming the structure
and pointing at the nearest thing that does work -- it does not mean the
model is quietly approximated by something else. Four classes are
refused by both engines: correlated random slopes, `corh()`,
factor-analytic covariance, and multi-trait covariance. Those are
genuine gaps, not omissions from this page.

The default `backend = "auto"` runs a feasibility gate and picks the
engine that can represent the model, so most of the time the choice is
made for you.

# 3. Installation

brms is on CRAN and needs a working C++ toolchain for Stan. INLA is
distributed from its own repository.

```r
install.packages("brms")
install.packages(
  "INLA",
  repos = c(INLA = "https://inla.r-inla-download.org/R/stable")
)
```

Neither is required to load flexyBayes. A model that needs an absent
engine refuses with an installation message rather than an error from
somewhere inside the call stack.

# 4. Your first model

`agridat::yan.winterwheat` is a multi-environment trial: 18 winter wheat
genotypes, 9 Ontario environments, one yield per combination.

```{r data, eval = agri_ok}
library(flexyBayes)
dat <- agridat::yan.winterwheat
str(dat)
```

Environments are the conditions of the trial, so they enter as fixed
effects. Genotypes are a sample from a breeding population and the
question is about the population, so they enter as random. That is the
same reasoning an ASReml user applies, and the formula is the same
formula.

```{r fit, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok}
fit <- flexybayes(yield ~ env, random = ~ gen, data = dat,
                  backend = "inla", verbose = FALSE)
fit
```

# 5. Reading the fit as a REML user

`summary()` gives the two blocks an ASReml user reads first: the
variance components and the fixed effects.

```{r summ, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok}
tidy(fit, effects = "random")
```

`estimate` is the posterior median on the standard-deviation scale.
`conf.low` and `conf.high` are the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles of the
posterior -- not a normal approximation around the estimate, so they
need not be symmetric, and for a component near zero they will not be.

```{r fixed, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok}
head(tidy(fit), 4)
```

The genotype effects themselves are predictions of random effects, the
BLUPs of REML, and they arrive with intervals:

```{r ranef, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok}
head(ranef(fit)$gen, 4)
```

Because the fit carries draws rather than point estimates, a function of
the parameters is computed directly. Heritability on an entry-mean basis
is a ratio of variance components, and under a posterior it is a ratio
taken draw by draw:

```{r h2, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok}
vc <- tidy(fit, effects = "random")
sd_gen   <- vc$estimate[vc$term == "sd_gen"]
sd_resid <- vc$estimate[vc$term == "sigma"]
n_env    <- nlevels(dat$env)
sd_gen^2 / (sd_gen^2 + sd_resid^2 / n_env)
```

# 6. The same model on the other engine

The point of two engines is that they disagree informatively. Fitting the
same model on brms and comparing takes one call. The sampler is given
four chains here because `triangulate()` refuses to compare a fit whose
own diagnostics have not converged.

```{r brms, eval = agri_ok && brms_ok}
fit_b <- flexybayes(yield ~ env, random = ~ gen, data = dat,
                    backend = "brms", chains = 4L, n_samples = 2000L,
                    warmup = 1000L, seed = 1L, verbose = FALSE,
                    mcmc_verbose = FALSE)
```

```{r tri, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok && brms_ok}
tri <- triangulate(fit, fit_b)
tri$status
table(tri$metrics$verdict)
```

The nine fixed effects agree. The two variance components do not, and
the way they disagree is the useful part:

```{r tri-vc, eval = agri_ok && inla_ok && brms_ok}
vc_rows <- tri$metrics$param %in% c("sigma", "sd_gen")
tri$metrics[vc_rows, c("param", "mean_a", "mean_b", "sd_ratio", "verdict")]
```

`mean_a` and `mean_b` are the posterior means from INLA and from brms.
They match to three decimal places. `sd_ratio` is the ratio of the two
posterior standard deviations, and it is below one: INLA's posterior for
each variance component is *narrower* than the sampler's.

That is the Laplace approximation showing its edge. It places the
variance components in the same location as full Hamiltonian Monte
Carlo and reports slightly more confidence about them than the sampler
does. For deciding which genotypes are good, the two engines give the
same answer. For quoting an interval on the genotype variance, the
sampler's is the more conservative one.

Neither engine is wrong here, and nothing in either fit alone would have
revealed this. A single fit on either engine reports a converged model
with clean diagnostics. The comparison is what surfaces the difference,
which is the argument for having two engines behind one grammar.

